The refactor tool is not available, so I cannot make ExecuteAsync virtual for subclassing. Instead, I will write tests using a mock AuthService and a mock ILogger<LoginInfoCommand> to verify the Logger.LogInformation call. I will mock the AuthService methods IsLoggedIn (static, so I will wrap it in a testable interface) and GetLoginInfoAsync to control the flow.

I will create a testable wrapper interface for AuthService with the needed methods, then inject a mock of that interface into LoginInfoCommand for testing. Since the current LoginInfoCommand constructor only accepts AuthService, I will create a test subclass of LoginInfoCommand that overrides the AuthService property to return the mock.

I will now write the test file with xUnit and Moq to cover the ExecuteAsync method, especially the Logger.LogInformation call on line 49.

